Inventor: What This Does and Why

Phase A — VC Driver Forensics (RHEL 9 context)

The critical difference from DSL that Copilot missed: RHEL 9 uses udev — /dev is fully dynamic. There is no static device table. When /proc/tty/drivers shows unknown on a udev system, the driver_name field in struct tty_driver is genuinely NULL or empty. The script extracts the vc driver's symbol addresses from /proc/kallsyms and compares them against the expected kernel .text range (0xffffffff81000000 on x86_64). Any vc-related symbol outside that range points to injected code.

Phase B — ACM Device Siphoning

This is the high-value phase. An ACM device on an air-gapped machine with both adapters down has no business existing. The script sends AT interrogation commands (ATI, AT+CGMI, AT+COPS?, AT+CREG?, AT+CIMI) and captures the response stream. If you get back AT+COPS data showing a carrier name — that is a live cell modem registered to a network on a machine you believe is offline. AT+CIMI returns the IMSI of whatever SIM is driving it. Both are direct evidence.

Phase C — VC Hardening

Restricts /dev/vcs* to root:600 — closes the screen buffer exfil path. Unloads and blacklists uinput and mac_hid. Adds auditd watches on /dev/console, /dev/tty0, /dev/vcs, and module load events. Any process touching the console surface after this generates an audit log entry.

Phase D — rfkill block all

Applied immediately. Both wireless adapters soft-blocked at the kernel level. PHY-layer attack surface reduced while Phase 2 wireless hardening is built.

